            "text": "Passes positive values unchanged and bends negative values along an exponential that levels off at minus alpha: alpha times (e to the x over alpha, minus 1).\n\nIt is elu with the exponential stretched by alpha, so the curve leaves 0 with slope 1 whatever alpha is - the two sides always join smoothly, with no corner. (\"Continuously differentiable\": the slope never jumps.)\n\nTHE CURVE:\nOn the right, the input itself. On the left, a curve that starts along the diagonal and levels off at -alpha. A small alpha bends it over sharply and close to zero (alpha 0.25 floors at -0.25, close to relu with a rounded corner); a large alpha follows the diagonal further before levelling off. At alpha 1 it is identical to elu.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.nn.celu\nNo arguments; default alpha 1.0.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor to bend. Receiving it triggers the node. A list, number or numpy array is turned into a float32 tensor on the cpu first; a tensor arriving as a tensor keeps its own dtype and device. The curve is applied to every element on its own, so the output has the same shape as the input. An INTEGER tensor (t.arange with whole numbers makes one) is refused: the console reports \"not implemented for 'Long'\" and nothing is sent. Put a t.to float32 in front of it.\n\nalpha (drag float on the node, and an inlet):\nThe depth of the floor, and how far the curve follows the diagonal before it bends. Default 1.0. Alpha 0 is not allowed (it divides by alpha): the console reports \"alpha cannot be 0 for CELU\" and nothing is sent, which is why the demo slider starts at 0.1. It does not trigger the node: the next tensor uses it. The demo's metro re-sends the ramp ten times a second, so the curve follows the slider.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe bent tensor, the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nt.linspace makes 128 values running evenly from -4 (left edge of the plot) to 4 (right edge); the middle of the plot is 0. The plot draws two lines over each other: the straight diagonal is the input itself, and the other is the node's output for the same values. Where the two lines coincide the node passes the value unchanged; where they part, that is the bend. The plot's height runs from -4 to 4; the input line runs off the top and bottom where it leaves that range.\n\nRELATED:\nt.nn.elu is the same at alpha 1 and has a corner at other values. t.nn.selu is elu with fixed constants. t.nn.softplus is another relu without a corner, one that never goes below zero."
        },
